@@ -23,6 +23,8 @@ A **seam** is the public boundary you test at: the interface where you observe b
 
 Ask: "What's the public interface, and which seams should we test?" Give each proposed seam a one-line note on what it catches and what it misses.
 
+Pre-agreed seams can come from an earlier agreement instead of a fresh question. If the ticket or spec you are implementing has a **Proof** line (see `to-tickets`) or names its seams, those are the agreed seams. Inside `/poteto-mode`, or when the user has stepped away, don't stop to ask: pick the seams, list them with their catches/misses notes in your report, and let the user course-correct.
+
 When the shape of that interface is itself in question (how deep the module is, where the seam belongs, what the interface should expose), call the Skill tool with "codebase-design" for the vocabulary. It is the shared source of the module, interface, depth, seam, adapter, leverage and locality terms, and it is a reference to consult, not a session to run.
 
 ## Anti-patterns
@@ -36,3 +38,11 @@ When the shape of that interface is itself in question (how deep the module is,
 - **Red before green.** Write the failing test first, then only enough code to pass it. Don't anticipate future tests or add speculative features.
 - **One slice at a time.** One seam, one test, one minimal implementation per cycle.
 - **Refactoring is not part of the loop.** It belongs to the review stage (see the `code-review` skill), not the red → green implementation cycle.
+
+## When a failing test is impractical
+
+Prefer no new test over a bad test. If the only available test would need broad harness setup, brittle mocks, production-only state, or would be deleted right after proving the fix, skip it and use the closest executable check instead: a targeted script, a reproduction command, browser automation, or a log assertion. Say which one you used and why.
+
+## Report the evidence
+
+Name the test (or check) that went red, the failure it produced, and the run where it went green. If you couldn't show red-before-green, say why and what you ran instead.
